course: dictionary
lesson: 6932
title: One word, many places
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A word can mean different things in different places, and the context tells you which meaning is meant. «At the zoo the African savanna area is one big grassy paddock where giraffes, zebras and antelopes wander together.» Here the animals roam across a big grassy field, so a paddock is a field.
explain: In Badger's warning, «a hundred badgers with rifles would attack from the paddock». Rifles and an attack need plenty of room, so the paddock must be a field. Check it by swapping in "field": the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Ask where the story happens. In Australia and New Zealand a paddock is any field on a farm, even a very big one. In Britain it is usually a small field for horses, often near a house or stable. At a racecourse, the paddock is where horses or cars are kept and shown before a race.
fact: A paddock is a field where animals are kept. In Britain it is usually a small one for horses, often near a house or stable.
fact: In Australia and New Zealand, a paddock is any field on a farm, even a very big one.
fact: At a racecourse or motor race, the paddock is the area where horses or cars are kept and shown before a race.
fact: «A few kangaroos loose in the top paddock» is an Australian saying for someone who is a bit muddled or silly.
fact: In maths, a paddock has a perimeter (the fence all the way round) and an area (the grass inside).
exercise: «At the zoo the African savanna area is one big grassy paddock where giraffes, zebras and antelopes wander together.» What is a paddock here? | A small room for lions | A racetrack for cars | A kind of zoo building | *A large grassy field where animals roam
exercise: An Australian farmer says, «The sheep are out in the back paddock.» Where are the sheep? | In a stable | Inside the farmhouse | *In a field on the farm | At the racecourse
exercise: A maths question asks for the perimeter of a paddock. What is the perimeter? | The grass inside it | The gate into it | The number of horses in it | *The fence all the way around it
test: At a motor race, what is the paddock? | A road for the cars to drive on | A small pen for dogs | *The area where cars are kept and shown before the race | A tall fence around the track
test: Ben reads, «Badger warned them that a hundred badgers would attack from the paddock.» Which meaning fits best? | A small garden with flowers | *A field, big enough for an army of badgers | A kitchen | A river bank
exam: Which sentence uses paddock to mean a field on a farm? | The cars lined up in the paddock before the race | The cook made soup in the paddock | *The sheep are grazing in the back paddock | The bus stops at the paddock by the station
